/*
We are given a tree S.
We may construct a new tree T by adding vertices, with S being an induced subtree of T
(on the original vertices and original edges).
We need:
1) minimum possible colorfulness = minimum number of colors in a good coloring of T
2) among such T, minimum possible number of leaves

Good coloring:
For any two vertices of the same color, the rooted trees obtained by rooting T at those
vertices are isomorphic.

Key observation:
In any tree, vertices with the same rooted-tree isomorphism type can share a color, and
different types need different colors. So the minimum number of colors of T equals the
number of distinct rooted-tree isomorphism types appearing among vertices of T.

We may add vertices to make T highly symmetric. The minimum possible number of rooted types
for any nontrivial tree is exactly its radius+1 if the tree is "layer-uniform" from a center.
By adding vertices, we can always transform around a chosen center (vertex or edge midpoint)
so that all vertices at equal distance from the center have identical rooted type.
To contain S, the minimum achievable number of colors is exactly ceil(diameter(S)/2)+1.

Then among all such optimal constructions:
- If diameter is even, center is a vertex c, and optimal colors k = ecc(c)+1.
  To keep only k colors, every branch from c must be extended/truncated to the same depth R,
  where R = max distance from c among original vertices = radius.
  Minimal leaves are obtained by merging obligations by child-subtrees:
    For each neighbor-side of c that contains original vertices, we need recursively complete
    it to a uniform rooted tree of depth R-1 with minimum leaves.
  Different child subtrees of the center must be isomorphic, so the center needs
  m = max over child-sides of (minimum number of children required at depth 1 in that side).
  Total leaves = m * f(R-1, side_i) but this can be computed recursively.

A cleaner DP:
For a rooted original subtree constraint A (rooted at some node with parent fixed),
define need(u,p,h) = minimum number of leaves in a uniform rooted tree of exact height h
that contains the original rooted subtree of u away from p as an induced rooted subtree,
with u as the root.

Uniform rooted tree of height h means all vertices at same depth from root have identical
rooted type; equivalently root has t identical children, each root of a uniform tree of height h-1.

Transition:
- h must be at least the maximum original depth from u away from p.
- For h=0: possible iff u has no children away from p; leaves=1.
- For h>0:
    Let children of u away from p be v1..vk.
    Each child must fit into one of the identical child-subtrees of height h-1.
    Since child-subtrees are identical and original edges between different children don't exist,
    they can be embedded into distinct child copies. To minimize number of child copies, we can
    place at most one original child-root in each copy because root of a copy corresponds to one
    child of u. Thus number of copies t >= k.
    Also each copy must be able to host whichever original child assigned to it.
    Since copies are identical, they must all be capable of hosting the "hardest" child.
    Therefore choose t = max(k, 1 if k=0? actually if h>0 root may have children; to have exact
    height h, root needs at least 1 child).
    Minimal leaves = t * max_i need(vi,u,h-1), with convention if k=0 then 1 * perfect chain => 1 leaf.

Indeed if k=0 and h>0, minimal is a single child continuing height h-1, giving 1 leaf.

So:
if k=0: need=1 for any h>=0.
if k>0: need(u,p,h) = k * max_i need(vi,u,h-1) for h>0? Wait t must be at least k and all copies identical.
We can assign one original child per copy, and extra copies only increase leaves, so t=k.
Each copy must host one assigned child, but since copies identical, each copy must be able to host
the maximum-demand child. Hence leaves = k * max_i need(...).
Correct.

Now for whole tree:
Optimal number of colors C = floor((diam+2)/2)+? = ceil(diam/2)+1.

There may be one center vertex (even diam) or two centers edge (odd diam).
- Even diam: choose center c, answer leaves = need(c,-1,R), where R=diam/2.
- Odd diam: center is edge a-b, radius to midpoint is R+0.5, colors = R+2 where diam=2R+1.
  Then T should be symmetric around central edge. The two sides rooted at a away from b and at b away from a
  must be identical uniform rooted trees of height R containing respective constraints.
  Minimal leaves = 2 * max( need(a,b,R), need(b,a,R) ).

Need to compute heights feasibility. Since optimal h equals eccentricity from center side:
for even: R=radius, and for center c every original depth <=R.
for odd: each side depth from endpoint away from other <=R.

We'll compute need by memo DFS.
*/

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<vector<int>> g(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        --u; --v;
        g[u].push_back(v);
        g[v].push_back(u);
    }

    if (n == 1) {
        cout << 1 << " " << 1 << "\n";
        return 0;
    }

    auto bfs = [&](int s) {
        vector<int> dist(n, -1), par(n, -1);
        queue<int> q;
        q.push(s);
        dist[s] = 0;
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front(); q.pop();
            for (int v : g[u]) if (dist[v] == -1) {
                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1;
                par[v] = u;
                q.push(v);
            }
        }
        int far = s;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) if (dist[i] > dist[far]) far = i;
        return tuple<int, vector<int>, vector<int>>(far, dist, par);
    };

    auto [a0, d0, p0] = bfs(0);
    auto [b, da, pa] = bfs(a0);
    auto [tmp, db, pb] = bfs(b);

    int diam = da[b];

    vector<int> path;
    for (int x = b; x != -1; x = pa[x]) path.push_back(x);
    reverse(path.begin(), path.end()); // a0 ... b

    // memo for need(u,p,h)
    // key by directed edge index + h; parent=-1 only for center case.
    map<tuple<int,int,int>, long long> memo;

    function<long long(int,int,int)> need = [&](int u, int p, int h) -> long long {
        auto key = make_tuple(u, p, h);
        auto it = memo.find(key);
        if (it != memo.end()) return it->second;

        vector<int> ch;
        for (int v : g[u]) if (v != p) ch.push_back(v);

        long long ans;
        if (h == 0) {
            ans = ch.empty() ? 1LL : (long long)4e18; // infeasible if has children
        } else {
            if (ch.empty()) {
                ans = 1; // extend by a chain
            } else {
                long long mx = 0;
                for (int v : ch) {
                    long long cur = need(v, u, h - 1);
                    mx = max(mx, cur);
                }
                ans = mx * (long long)ch.size();
            }
        }
        memo[key] = ans;
        return ans;
    };

    long long leaves;
    int colors = (diam + 1) / 2 + 1; // ceil(diam/2)+1

    if (diam % 2 == 0) {
        int R = diam / 2;
        int c = path[R];
        leaves = need(c, -1, R);
    } else {
        int R = diam / 2;
        int x = path[R], y = path[R + 1];
        long long L = need(x, y, R);
        long long Rv = need(y, x, R);
        leaves = 2LL * max(L, Rv);
    }

    cout << colors << " " << leaves << "\n";
    return 0;
}

